campaignTrail_temp.answer_feedback_json = JSON.parse("[{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950001,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940001,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You sit down at your computer, pouring everything you have into a full-page ad in the newspaper, with a headline using the line from Network, “I’M MAD AS HELL, AND I’M NOT GONNA TAKE IT ANYMORE”. You scatter the ad across your home of Tampa. You can’t help but think to yourself that this is the beginning of something special as you send the ad out.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950002,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940002,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"No one is brave enough, Jack…..\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950003,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940003,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Given your position as a normal civilian, your words about President Reagan carry very little weight towards the opinion of the nostalgic public. You come out of your first media circuit netting next to nobody, save for a few disillusioned libertarians and some radicals on both sides of the spectrum. St. Pete Times declares you an “Icarus” a day later.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950004,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940004,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"TIME sings your praises a short time later, as their “Hero of the week” for your efforts. Tens of thousands more enthusiastic letters pour in from across the country, some simply addressed “Jack Gargan, Tampa!” You later learn from a friend that Nancy Kassebaum has deemed you “The Most Feared and Hated Man on Capitol Hill”. You revel in the honor.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950005,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940005,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Though it isn’t the most electrifying issue, this angle helps solidify your campaign as being legitimate, not simply a protest vote. The voters aren’t ready to bust down a door for you, but quietly, the independents listen, wondering if this will finally be the time they can come out of the woodworks.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950006,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940006,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Perot, in his high-pitched Texan drawl, expectedly asks how he can help out with your movement. You tell him there isn’t much he can do, and that you’re done with the new ads you had planned. Perot understands, and offers his answer whenever you need anything else from him. You’ve just made your first crucial ally.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950007,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940007,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Perot seems passively agitated as you talk briefly with the man for a few minutes. You try to ask him about his time saving P.O.W s but he responds coldly “we can talk about that another time”. Though Perot seemed open to future talks, you gave a poor first impression to the billionaire, leaving you in a peculiar position.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950008,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940008,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The many elected officials of America tremble after you run a full-page ad in USA Today declaring “PSSST, CONGRESS, I’M B-A-A-A-C-K!”. However, donations are sparse, due to the nature of USA Today’s readership, leaving you disheartened with another loss of money that you are beginning to run out of.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950009,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940009,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You buy a massive Dodge truck for 20,000 dollars, dubbing the car the “THROMOBILE” and hit the road for the first time, without anyone with you. Driving hundreds of miles a day every day, sleeping in shitty motels every night, giving impassioned town hall speeches in every city worth a damn, from the hicktowns of the deep south to the working-class midwest. It's far from the gaudy stadiums and convention centers that your opposition use, but you’re getting your point across where it matters.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950010,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940010,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's a hard life for you. Constantly you are forced to appear on radio stations early in the morning. You groggily talk about T.H.R.O, term limits, and the economy, and it's far from what people in rush hour traffic want to hear. Your radio circuit draws few, and nets even fewer supporters.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950011,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940011,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You throw out a bunch of potential running mates but Perot shoots each one of them down. When you mention Barbara Jordan, “Jordan’s got health problems, you know, and we’re from the same state”, and when you throw out Norman Schwartzkopf, “They don’t call him stormin’ Norman for nothing, Jack”. This gives you everything you need, he really is interested.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950012,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940012,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Ross smirks, “Jack, I’m a man of action. When I see a poisonous snake, I don’t call a committee meeting, I shoot it!”. You are not disheartened by this answer and keep prying the billionaire about various things outside of politics. The meeting reaches the hour mark, then stretches to two hours, cutting into Perot’s next appointment. When you walk out into the scorching Texas heat, you feel momentum starting to build.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950013,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940013,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Ross, of course, flatly denies any interest in running for president, though his rebuke of the offer is not as conclusive as you had previously thought. You have a pleasant talk with him anyways, before he politely asks you to leave once the hour mark is reached. He’s definitely a tough nut to crack, but you know you’ll be able to get him eventually.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950014,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940014,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Just so the history books get this right, it is four twenty-six in the afternoon on June first, nineteen ninety-one that the ‘Draft Perot For President’ movement began”. The Kansas City Star reports the next morning MOVEMENT STARTED TO DRAFT PEROT<br><br>You call Ross, letting him know.<br><br>The floodgates have opened<br><br>America will never be the same again\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950015,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940015,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Just so the history books get this right, it is four twenty-six in the afternoon on June first, nineteen ninety-one that the ‘Draft Perot For President’ movement began”. The Kansas City Star reports the next morning MOVEMENT STARTED TO DRAFT PEROT<br><br>You call Ross, letting him know.<br><br>The floodgates have opened<br><br>America will never be the same again\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950016,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940016,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Just so the history books get this right, it is four twenty-six in the afternoon on June first, nineteen ninety-one that the ‘Draft Perot For President’ movement began”. The Kansas City Star reports the next morning MOVEMENT STARTED TO DRAFT PEROT<br><br>You call Ross, letting him know.<br><br>The floodgates have opened<br><br>America will never be the same again\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950017,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940017,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"North cannot make it, to your chagrin, leaving you with a bunch of second rate conservative speakers headed by U.N Ambassador Alan Keyes and the founder of the Constitution Party. They both give dry, standard speeches that leave you in a daze by the end of them. Perot, of course, gives a great speech, the room exploding in chants of “Run, Ross, Run!” when he finishes. He says he doesn’t want to run, but how can he say that now?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950018,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940018,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The event goes awry when author L. Neil Smith began ranting about how bad George Washington was as a president, and advertised his books in the process. Horrified, you politely ask him to leave, but not before Perot storms out, telling you “if this is the movement you want, then we need to talk some more”.<br><br>Shit.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950019,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940019,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Anderson agrees, and his opening speech is greatly supportive, but also warns the many independents of America that this can easily fall apart. Perot slams it out of the park, commanding the hotel room with the same swagger that had initially drawn you to him all those years ago. The crowd erupts in applause, a woman even fainting from excitement. Perot’s speech seemed awfully campaign-like, making you wonder what his true intentions are…\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950020,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940020,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The congressman gives a fantastic speech, harkening back to speeches made by Henry Wallace about protecting the American farmer, something that Reagan, Bush, and the Democratic Party have failed to do. This is immediately followed by Perot’s speech, giving an enthusiastic defense of everything your movement has stood for, and you get a picture with him afterwards, he leaves before you can talk about future plans.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950021,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940021,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Well, number one I don’t want to. Number two, if you’re that serious, if you, the people, are that serious? You register me in 50 states, and if you’re not willing to organize, this is all just talk.”<br><br>You’ve done it.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950022,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940022,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Immediately, your phone number gets thousands of calls, overwhelming your office’s telephone operators. Overnight, your loyal employees turn your building into a campaign headquarters, a cathedral of a movement long overdue for the American public. You are forced to put your business operations on hold as a result.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950023,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940023,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"A slew of advisors are hired, but your big break comes when Ed Rollins and Hamilton Jordan from the Reagan and Carter administrations, who both helped build winning campaigns, join your fight. You can’t help but roll your eyes, realizing that you are succumbing to the very same practices that the establishment uses.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950024,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940024,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your militia of volunteers move like locusts across a wheatfield, mobilized to defend their country from the tyranny that gripped them during all the years of Reagan. They petition and advertise at a speed unlike anything you ever saw them do at work. Tennessee falls first, with dozens more soon to come.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950025,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940025,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Luce gets moving immediately. He sets up a meeting with Gargan, finding, to your dismay, that Gargan set up a correspondence office in Washington on his own. He attempts to smoke the operation out but Gargan fiendishly stands his ground, quickly becoming a headache for everyone involved. You wish you hadn’t agreed to meet with that loser.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950026,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940026,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"America has known this about you for a long time, and it hurts you a bit. Desert Storm, no matter how you spin it, was popular, making your non-interventionist foreign policy an anathema to the very Reaganite majority, who gawk at the thought of reducing America’s military might abroad.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950027,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940027,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Calling for a return to simpler times is an interesting move, and one that pays off tremendously. An entire generation of Americans who grew up in the shining postwar years dream for a return to the days of their youth when politicians cared, and people were nicer to each other. So they say, of course.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950028,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940028,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"At first, this strategy proved to be effective, swaying thousands of disaffected voters across the country. But slowly, your statements fall apart with a bit of prying, and it all culminates in an appearance on NBC Today where you begin talking about how we can save a fortune by cutting social security for the wealthy. It's a bad look, something you have never experienced in your life.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950029,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940029,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"To an entire generation of Americans scarred by everything from Watergate to Iran-Contra,  this message hits home. A statement you make on C-SPAN, calling the president “A creature of Nixon” scares many into bolting from Bush to you, making your tent bigger, and your room for error smaller. You pray nobody will uncover your quarter of a million dollars donated straight to the CRP back in the early 70s.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950030,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940030,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Communists, fascists, libertarians, and other various kooks call you hoping to get their big break in politics, all of which you easily brush off. After dozens of calls, you feel like giving up.  But, moments before you head to bed, a call comes in from California. Five minutes later, your shortlist expands by one.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950031,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940031,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"This statement leaves you satisfied as you head home that night, your mind going as fast as your car on the 35-E figuring out what businessman might be up for serving as second-banana to you. When you get home, a man you had long considered for this job calls you, expressing his interest. You excitedly write his name down on the growing list of people you want for the job.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950032,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940032,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You rack your brain for hours trying to find a politician that isn’t bought and still knows how to run a competent administration. Eventually, one comes to mind, and after a short talk with the man, he agrees to be considered, giving yourself a much needed win in your search for the presidency.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950033,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940033,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You make a fiery statement the next morning, ranting on all the horrible things Bush did to Dukakis, and how you are being treated the same. Since you only entered politics two months ago, this response falls on deaf ears. Bush gains sympathy points later that night when he plays up his long career serving the world, and how he would “never do such a thing”.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950034,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940034,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Cool and collected, you confidently defend yourself from the recent attacks, and you not only prevent a bleed of supporters, but also allow your army of supporters to continue your fight as usual without your reputation being tarnished. It was a close call, but it would seem you survived your first ever political attack.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950035,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940035,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"And just like that, you become a joke. The national media is quick to make fun of you, dominating any story about Bush or Clinton all week. Matt Groening, cartoonist of The Simpsons fame, later makes fun of you in his comic Life In Hell, portraying you as an insane conspiracy theorist. You silently embrace the moniker.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950036,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940036,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Though questions begin to surround you about your obsessiveness, this strategy generally works. However, Clinton brings the scandal up again a week later at a town hall, throwing a minor dent into your shiny image that you spend so much time trying to keep up. You keep having to remind yourself that this is what the people want, but it becomes too much to bear.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950037,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940037,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You had the best of intentions, but you forgot to take into account that you don’t have any credentials with the minority communities in America. You try to come off as sincere as possible, but it comes off as a stilted, and uninspiring attempt to appeal to the downtrodden. Clinton later comes to Los Angeles and knocks it out of the park. Bastard.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950038,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940038,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You descend into the hell the City of Angels created, each street you drive by making you sick to your stomach. You help dozens of families, giving them food and other supplies, and help rebuild the small businesses that were looted. While you’ve gained the respect of the people you helped, the rest of the country is wondering why you didn’t say anything, assuming you are just another heartless billionaire. It hurts you, but you keep your dignity.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950039,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940039,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You see Governor Clinton on the TV set in your office, espousing his authentic southern charm to appeal to the many affected by the crisis. He speaks like a snake oil salesman, spinning empty platitudes about helping our minorities faster than anyone you had ever seen. The news adorns him with praise, leaving your weak attacks at President Bush in the dust.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950040,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940040,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Bush and Clinton both travel to Los Angeles, leaving you ample opportunity to once again assault the airwaves with your anti-establishment rhetoric. Like every other time, it sticks to voters, but many of them wonder why you aren’t there too, giving aid to the ones who need it most.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950041,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940041,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Americans care about loyalty more than anything. They remember Nixon, they remember Iran-Contra, they get the message. Clinton’s team is flabbergasted, and hastily put out a half-hearted response that doesn’t seem to gain them any real boosts in the polls, which you continue to climb in.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950042,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940042,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your team gets caught in the act, and a scathing criticism of your actions is published in The Washington Post, making you a national laughingstalk for prying too deep into Clinton’s personal life. Clinton’s campaign releases an ad where you are “big little brother” to mock your short stature, halting the momentum built by your volunteers.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950043,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940043,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"And yet, the Comeback Kid keeps coming back, cranking his southern charm up to the max and wriggling out of everything thrown at him. Bruised from a tough primary campaign, the true underdog in this race keeps persevering, nearly catching up to you and Bush in the polls. It appears the Democrats won’t go down as easily as you had hoped.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950044,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940044,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"People do hold on to your every word on the economy, but Luce nearly punches you in the face that night. It was an easy opportunity to claim the moral high ground that you chose not to exploit. He tells you that claiming moral high grounds is what politics is all about, but did he forget the whole reason you are running?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950045,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940045,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"A day after you talk about the importance of American superiority on the global stage, Jack calls you. He can smell the blood in the water, wondering what has made you change your rhetoric. You tell him that you are attempting to get Buchanan to endorse you. The phone line goes dead immediately. What is your movement without its creator?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950046,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940046,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Buchanan’s demagogic crusade for taxpaying rights keeps moving without your help. He secures a few more delegates, and a few more votes, but ultimately, his flame expectedly burns out. You reach out to him in secret a few days later, and he expresses interest in a cabinet position, giving you a much-needed advisor for your future presidency.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950047,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940047,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The people want the whale itself, and not its biology. With each passing day, your points become weaker and weaker to an increasingly bored public. Rollins and Jordan call you every day, begging you to hear them out, but you rarely ever pick up, much to their dismay. It's your campaign, not theirs.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950048,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940048,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"One week you talk to AIDS survivors. The next you’re at a rally with various third party chairs, and the week after that the campaign trots out Warren Rudman and Sam Nunn to talk about defense and the budget, respectively. It doesn’t make any sense to you as to why you are sidelined in favor of the people you swear to take down.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950049,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940049,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"And so, for virtually the first time, you hit the trail. You first go to Gary, Indiana, in front of a dilapidated factory that once stood tall before the trade policies of the Reagan administration got to it. The workers of the midwest listen to you under the smog, their tired, lifeless eyes filling your soul with sorrow.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950050,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940050,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The public is fascinated by your credentials, including daring business stories and your trips to Vietnam to save P.O.W ’s. It's a nice story for people to hear, but they care the most about your opinions on issues. Though you’ve cleared up your reputation, it doesn’t do much to nudge your polling numbers any farther\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950051,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940051,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You put on your best Pat Buchanan impression on the trail, calling out Bush’s economic policies, seeing your soundbites go live to the whole nation, to your disgust. Saturday Night Live mocks you later, as a little kid trying to present to his parents. It's a sorry sight to see.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950052,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940052,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"No president has managed to balance the budget since Johnson, and it's true that the people yearn for another year of surplus, but your calls for a balanced budget are hardly unique. Clinton, in an attempt to gain back lost ground, has begun making balancing the budget an issue himself, undercutting your stances. Bastard.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950053,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940053,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's popular with people who are dumb enough to think it's a good idea, but a flat tax would never actually be passed. Like the politicians you seek to destroy, you too are blowing hot air in an attempt to win the people over. Bush later jokes about you wanting “Tax communism” on the trail. It sounds good on paper, but it would never work in practice.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950054,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940054,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You are greeted by thousands of cheering and smiling latinos as you give your speech. Surprisingly, they listen, and are captivated by you, almost missing your gaffe calling them “beaners”. Almost. It's a setback, but you seem to have netted a decent chunk of the population down there. If only you hadn’t fucked it up.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950055,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940055,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The sprawl of Brooklyn astonishes you as you speak before a Jewish congregation. You start in a rhythm, speaking about Clinton not being able to understand them. The whole thing goes to shit when someone screams “you hate us!” because of your connections to Jesse Jackson. Embarrassed, you head back to Dallas, and the Jews of New York are not pleased,\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950056,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940056,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"When speaking about blacks being bit first by economic downturn, you refer to them as “you people”. In hours, the news is all over it. When black leaders are asked, they say you aren’t racist, you just don’t know anything about them. The story catches fire nationally, and your polling numbers sink back down.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950057,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940057,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The minorities of America cry out still, with Clinton and Bush offering a fake hand to help them. You’d rather not bother with the whole charade of promising the world for them. You begin a tour of the suburbs of America, appealing to their pearl-clutching, middle class backgrounds, red meat for them, and you once again neglect those who truly need help.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950058,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940058,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Millions of dedicated Americans sit open mouthed as they watch the announcement, the buzz from it stealing the thunder of Clinton’s acceptance speech. You say a bunch of lies “if the election were held, it would be a deadlock, and I don’t want that” yes you do. The people know you do. You rant for a few minutes longer about President Bush, then go silent.<br><br>Washington weeps.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950059,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940059,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You stand headstrong against the torrent, and fire Rollins and Jordan personally. They tell you that politics is war, or as close to it as you’ll ever find. You brush off what they say, as usual, and tell them to go out. In their place, you hire known yes-man Orson Swindle. You officially announce your candidacy that day, taking eyes off Bill Clinton’s speaking time during the DNC. For the first time since 1912, a true 3 way race has begun.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950060,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940060,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Voters are confused. What did you mean by “another authoritarian regime”? The media speculates for hours, until you appear and say that you meant the United States, a country that only ever had one more serious option than Germany in 1936. Clinton fires back, saying that “one more is enough”.<br><br>No it isn’t.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950061,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940061,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's a perfectly acceptable stance, but your answer comes across as being oddly dull and robotic in a campaign filled with one-liners and straight talk. Clinton laughs at this position, saying, “well, I guess he’s finally come around to something reasonable”. He’s the last person you want any praise from.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950062,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940062,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Suburbanites who have never met a black person nearly faint with excitement. The last thing they want is their child getting involved with the wrong crowd, not like anything like that will ever get to them. Drug use has infected everything their children consume from song lyrics to movies. Reagan and Nixon failed, but this time, you will succeed.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950063,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940063,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The cities burn and decay another day. People are left stranded, homeless, victims of violent crime cry out for your help, but it never comes, not only from you, but from everyone. All you have proved to them is just how scummy the world can truly be, and how America is a sick and twisted place.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950064,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940064,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"No one has cared about internal improvements since Henry Clay was alive, and your support of them won’t change that. Your statement is lambasted across the country, as Clinton wryly remarks “I guess Perot read Man In The High Castle and wished it became reality”. Christ, don’t make generalizations like that again.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950065,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940065,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your past haunts you again, and you pay the price for it. Editorials come out like clockwork from various allies of the duopoly, questioning your fitness for the presidency, and finally, the people start to listen to them. Clinton’s the hot new thing, you’re just old stuff, conspiring about something many had forgotten about years ago.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950066,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940066,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Very presidential-sounding of you, Ross. Unfortunately, this is essentially identical to what Clinton is peddling. Plagiarism accusations are thrown at you immediately and you are forced to make a fiery denouncement on live TV. Your advisors collectively bang their heads against a wall and question why they still work for you.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950067,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940067,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's a cute thought to have, but the people who want to vote for you are repulsed at the thought of another boring president who won’t actually listen to the very people that got them there. 4 more years of Bush’s foreign policy or 4 years of Clinton’s aren’t what they are looking for in a candidate,\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950068,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940068,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You are greeted by tens of thousands at a rally in Pittsburgh. They hail you as the first candidate who stands up for the little guy since FDR. The local steel union even chooses to endorse you over Clinton, saying that you are “the only candidate who knows what he’s talking about”. You laugh. You have absolutely no idea what you are talking about.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950069,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940069,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's a unique approach. Clinton and Bush brush it off as nothing more than noise, but silently, the rust belt listens, entranced by the thought of their cities returning to the same importance they had once held when they were growing up. You earn extremely muted praise from a few city officials across the midwest.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950070,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940070,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“It's an investment worth making” you declare to a crowd in Boston, but the people listening seem unenthused. They don’t want another 500 billion dollar project on something that might fail. 30 years ago, your challenge would have been a rallying cry, but now, it's just another empty promise.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950071,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940071,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“The World of Tomorrow”, to voters, had long been portrayed as a dark, bleak time that would bring about the end of civilization. But with your cheery pitch of the newfangled internet, their thoughts begin to shift. The World of Tomorrow seems like a light at the end of a multi-generational tunnel of horrors, and despite Clinton’s youth, they see you as that light.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950072,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940072,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You almost choke on the last sentence. You remember why you didn’t want to run for president; it's because of the bureaucracy of it all. A task force won’t do anything, and you’ve served on enough of them in the business world to know why. It won’t help the people, so what is this whole thing even about, Ross?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950073,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940073,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Commerce? On a screen? It boggles the mind of an average midwestern family, but it makes sense to them. A future including that has been drilled into their head since they were watching The Jetsons on their TV sets, and they couldn’t be happier to see it unfold in front of them while enjoying the benefits of their unshakeable economy rising above the waves once again.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950074,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940074,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"To your amazement, a couple of them do, most notable of all being Jesse Jackson. Jackson gives a speech in Chicago about Clinton’s disdain for the black population and, while not endorsing you outright, says privately that he believes you to be the best candidate. He shakes hands with a young man after the speech, who said he was “a lifelong Democrat, but willing to break tradition just this once”.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950075,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940075,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's true that you’re talking about what the people want to hear, but at this point you sound like a broken record. You bore a crowd half to death at a stop in Topeka, your droning nearly as dry and desolate as the corn fields that occupy the rest of the state.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950076,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940076,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"In a stunning turn of events, you actually do something Jack approves of. The public has forgotten about the gross congressional gridlock, and reminding them of it energizes your base tenfold. Once again, the congressmen of America cower in fear at the thought of a person like Jack in the Oval Office\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950077,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940077,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Shockingly, 93% of 150,000 voters wanted you to run again. You pledged that you belonged to them, and on October 1st, you announced your intention to seek the presidency with James Stockdale as your running mate. Americans quietly celebrate everywhere, and you prepare for the upcoming debates that will decide the future of American democracy<br><br>The fight is on\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950078,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940078,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Well, I think they’ve got a point. I don’t have much experience in running up a 4 trillion dollar debt” you say defiantly. The crowd roars at your statement, and Bush and Clinton struggle to keep themselves upright as you cruise through their attempts to differentiate themselves from one another while you seem different without even trying.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950079,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940079,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Bush is up first, offering very little of substance on why he should be president at the same old age as his predecessor. For you, its target practice, and you destroy Bush on almost every issue. Clinton proves to be more of a challenge, but you avoid being dispatched by him the same way he dispatched Jerry Brown back in January. And somehow, you seemed the most presidential.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950080,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940080,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The networks deny your request instantly, with many in the Media Industrial Complex tired of your antics and are ready to finally see this election over with. Nevertheless, your performance against Clinton and Bush is strong, but you aren’t able to do anything that sets you apart from the others.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950081,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940081,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Clinton and Bush make much mention of the missing person at the third podium, and your campaign stops in the midwest are met with little fanfare. The media can’t get enough of it, either, poking fun at your history with MIAs, saying that you have gone away to go and join them.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950082,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940082,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Who am I? Why am I here?” Stockdale jokes, and you smack your head against your desk. Throughout, Stockdale appears like a doddering old retirement home escapee, and Gore makes short work of both him and Quayle, who once again loses the oratorical contest miserably.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950083,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940083,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"For as long as your campaign has been around, many haven’t taken you seriously. But the millions of Americans who have gotten this far are ready to prove them all wrong. With this final message, they have been galvanized, ready to rely on your every word once you are in the White House, and bring America truly back to the people, just like the founders intended.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950084,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940084,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"A good idea in theory, but in practice, it goes horribly. You abruptly storm out of a press conference in front of millions watching after being pressed too hard on your past with the Vietnam MIAs. Once again, your fitness for office is questioned, and your polling numbers sink into the abyss, sealing your ill-fated campaign to the dustbin of history.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950085,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940085,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your remarks in the earlier debate have become very popular, and the issue as a whole has become your biggest winning issue. Since NAFTA hasn’t been put into place, people will be willing to listen to any opinion on it, and with your charismatic approach towards it, they believe you the most. None of them want empty wallets, and certainly not to make the rich even richer.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950086,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940086,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You go over it all in an infomercial the next day. To some, it's puzzling, why would Bush want to start a new Cold War? But to a lot of people, it makes sense, this is the same party that prolonged Vietnam, that prevented the release of hostages, of course they would want to put America through another 40 years of war.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950087,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940087,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"New England welcomes you with open arms. The bastions of liberalism that it had once guarded now falter in defense of your movement. Your campaign ends in New Bedford, the same place where Captain Ahab had set sail to find his own White Whale. You hope you will find yours soon enough.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950088,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940088,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You speak in plenty of one-horse towns, each one of them so unique yet so devoid of life. Your final stop before heading back to Dallas is in Kansas City, the very place where Jack began his draft movement many months ago. You give a speech reminiscent of the one you had given a year ago, and the people love it.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950089,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940089,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your campaign ends where so many had begun, Detroit. You visit the old neighborhoods once considered beautiful. You stare at the lost industrial might of Ford’s Rouge Plant, as you speak before hundreds of auto workers. You have one last rally with Lee in the heart of the city. Each person there having hope in their eyes for the first time since the Eisenhower years.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950090,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940090,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Texas loves their homegrown boy a hell of a lot more than their President who pretends to be one of them. You excitedly rally in Tampa in front of thousands of enthusiastic supporters, but something feels off about the whole thing, and after a bit of thinking, you realize why that is.<br><br>Jack was nowhere to be found.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950091,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940091,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Ueberroth proves to be a rather boring speaker once he hits the trail, but is an effective person behind the scenes, securing endorsements and funding from different people he’s close with. Most notable of all is the billionaire owner of the Atlanta Braves, Ted Turner. His endorsement helps you greatly in his home state of Georgia, and you get a minor bump from those hippies in New England and California.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950092,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940092,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Silber seems good on paper; a fiscal conservative and a social liberal drawing the line at gay rights and assisted suicide. You are horrified, however, when he has an outburst on CNN a few days later about Bill Clinton not being “a true southerner”. You make enough gaffes on your own, Ross, do you really need more?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950093,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940093,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Hippies and Nazis alike cheer you on as you hold a rally with Brown in San Francisco. Brown gives a strong speech, citing that you were “the only way left to take America back” calling back to his time in the primaries. Across the bay, The Dead Kennedys hold a “Fight Fascism” charity concert with a few other local talents, reviled at the idea of a Jerry Brown Vice Presidency.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950094,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940094,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Lee’s platitudes about how he watched the auto industry fall from its glory atop his ivory perch overlooking Detroit’s decay resonate strongly. His populistic rage serves as a great compliment to yours, blanketing the Midwest in constant reminders of just how far they have fallen. As a result, your polling numbers spike there.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950095,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940095,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"The checks roll in from the tech buddies you made along the way, each giving a generous donation. You see donations from Jobs’ various connections he made at Apple, your old IBM friends, and many more. Around the country, pundits scratch their heads, but Steve is able to quickly draw young people towards your campaign, a demographic you were severely lacking in just a few weeks ago.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950096,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940096,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"None of them bite, and you are left with having to attack Clinton on your own, and the attacks don’t stick. He’s called “Slick Willy” for a reason, and you come out of the ad cycle helping the Governor, whose approval goes up, and with it, his polling. What the fuck is wrong with this country?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950097,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940097,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's true that you’re talking about what the people want to hear, but at this point you sound like a broken record. You bore a crowd half to death at a stop in Topeka, your droning nearly as dry and desolate as the corn fields that occupy the rest of the state.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950098,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940098,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"In a stunning turn of events, you actually do something Jack approves of. The public has forgotten about the gross congressional gridlock, and reminding them of it energizes your base tenfold. Once again, the congressmen of America cower in fear at the thought of a person like Jack in the Oval Office\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950099,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940099,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Pete gives a pretty solid defense of himself on 60 Minutes a few days later, and mostly puts the rumors to rest. Then, the president’s son is made the next MLB Commissioner and solves the whole thing, putting the spotlight back on the president as he boosts in the polls now that those old money bastards can watch their precious World Series again.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950100,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940100,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"To your chagrin, the Cowboys have a week 4 bye, but Jerry gets moving anyway. Seemingly without warning, the Raiders and Chiefs get bounced from the Monday Night slate in favor of a far better matchup between the 49ers and the Saints. Steve Young ends the day with 318 passing yards and 4 touchdowns in a thrilling win, but the sports junkies still ask where their MLB playoffs are. It never comes, and Ueberroth still shoulders the blame.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950101,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940101,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It doesn’t blow over. The strike lasts a week, then another, then another, with no end in sight. For the first time since its creation in 1903, there will be no World Series played, and with it, a small piece of American culture has died, all thanks to you.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950102,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940102,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Steve respects you enough to agree, but the simmering rage behind his eyes suggests otherwise. He tries his best to restrain himself, but he can’t really do it. The off the cuff statements go away, but it comes at the cost of much of Jobs’ flamboyance. He tells you not to go anywhere near NeXT. You laugh, but it didn’t seem like he was joking.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950103,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940103,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"After a long and intense argument, it seemed Steve might actually come around. But, after mentioning his ousting from Apple seven years ago, he is visibly shocked and appalled that you would ever bring it up around him. After more arguing, he bitterly leaves the ticket and spits on your shoe with a statement to the press afterward, leaving you with James Stockdale on the ticket again.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950104,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940104,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Jobs almost too happily takes the termination. You are forced to replace him with James Stockdale, the retired admiral you put on the ticket some months ago purely for taking up space. Your polling numbers plummet as a result.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950105,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940105,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Lee asks you if you have a sort of “Co-Presidency” in mind for the two of you. You retort that you haven’t really thought of it like that, but that in essence, he would be the Co-President. He takes solace in that, and you plan your next steps for November with him.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950106,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940106,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Well Ross, it's a great offer, but I’m willing to stick this thing out with you” he says. “I want to be the person who helps you through this thing, at least in your first hundred days. I’ll accept your offer in about a year, but for now we’re staying as a ticket, I don’t want to let you down”. Your fragile alliance continues.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950107,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940107,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Lee is disappointed, but understands your reasoning. He tells you that he didn’t really think this was gonna work out either. Lee wants to remain an American hero who saved multiple car companies, and not be your lapdog in Washington. In a few days, he is replaced as your running mate by James Stockdale, the dead weight you had assigned to the ticket to check a box.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950108,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940108,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Jeane’s defense works to an extent, but the accusations still linger. Although that chapter of history is now behind the country, most of them still fear the creeping threat of Socialism, and the ones that fear it the most desert your campaign in fear that their country may fall.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950109,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940109,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"For once in your campaign, your attack actually works. Bush puts on his nice face in front of the country, but they remain unconvinced. In a final blow, Richard Nixon himself crawls out of his New York apartment and confirms your allegations. He knows as much as you that it isn’t true at all, but he has nothing left to lose.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950110,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940110,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"You make a whistle-stop tour of Europe at a rate no one has ever seen before. You then attend a Jewish Service in Tel Aviv, the yamaka you put on your head ends up becoming world news, and becomes the new version of the Dukakis tank ad, paid for by one of Clinton’s billionaire cronies. You wish politics wasn’t this cruel.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950111,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940111,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"That is actually what Jerry meant by the statement, comparing Stalin to the Reagan administration’s supposed “strength”. But any praise of a genocidal dictator is praise nonetheless, and Brown is hounded over the fact that he would even think to compare a President to Joseph Stalin.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950112,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940112,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It makes you look indecisive, but ultimately, it was the right decision to make. Brown is humiliated out of politics and exiled back to California, where he will live out the rest of his days as a low-profile political consultant. In other news, The Dead Kennedys released their single Fuhrerbunker to celebrate Brown’s political execution.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950113,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940113,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Jerry gives a solid defense of his record as Governor and as a presidential candidate, saying that he would never praise a guy like Stalin. But the thought of his statement still sticks in the back of voters' minds, perhaps being their deciding factor in not voting for you. You push on, bruised and bloodied, to November.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950114,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940114,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Did you really expect it to work? Silber angrily puts his “Silber Shockers” on display once again, making a general fool of himself and discrediting your campaign beyond belief. You would see a lot of you in him if you weren’t blind to your own imperfections.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950115,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940115,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Once again, politics’ court jester has to defend its own actions in front of its king. They don’t take you seriously like they used to. Term limits on congress can come later, on a better kempt vessel. They all need to jump ship, but the gleam from the horizon in your eye tells you not to let them move a muscle, as New Bedford drifts farther and farther away.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950116,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940116,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Your numbers dip the longer and longer this scandal goes uncommented on, but it's a sacrifice you are willing to make. Clinton continues lying through his teeth everywhere he goes, but, it will all come back to haunt him, right?\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950117,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940117,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“Well, I think they’ve got a point. I don’t have much experience in running up a 4 trillion dollar debt” you say defiantly. The crowd roars at your statement, and Bush and Clinton struggle to keep themselves upright as you cruise through their attempts to differentiate themselves from one another while you seem different without even trying.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950118,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940118,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Bush is up first, offering very little of substance on why he should be president at the same old age as his predecessor. For you, its target practice, and you destroy Bush on almost every issue. Clinton proves to be more of a challenge, but you avoid being dispatched by him the same way he dispatched Jerry Brown back in January. And somehow, you seemed the most presidential.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950119,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940119,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Shockingly, your stunt becomes the main attraction and you blow Americans away by being able to debate without being there physically. Newspapers around the country declare you the true winner, and any buzz on what Clinton and Bush said is overshadowed by your charts, which, unlike politicians, can never lie.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950120,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940120,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Clinton and Bush make much mention of the missing person at the third podium, and your campaign stops in the midwest are met with little fanfare. The media can’t get enough of it, either, poking fun at your history with MIAs, saying that you have gone away to go and join them.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950121,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940121,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Jobs looks like a kid in a candy store. Gore and Quayle try as hard as possible to steer the debate away from what Jobs knows, but Steve takes command and wows people with his knowledge of technology and how it will be used in politics in the future. He gets visibly ticked off at the fact that an idiot like Dan Quayle could comprehend anything he does, however.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950122,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940122,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Silber, the loose cannon that he is, goes on the offensive, and it pays off in some ways and bites you in others. Silber is able to attack Gore and Quayle while the two attack him right back. It's a bloody affair, and at the end of 90 minutes, nobody won, and most viewers at home were left unhappy as they switch their TV to watch Homer Simpson get another wacky job halfway through.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950123,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940123,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Kirkpatrick’s debating skills aren’t much more than a boring lull, mixing with Gore’s into a thick smog of bureaucracy. To everyone's surprise, probably even to the President’s surprise, Quayle actually slips away unharmed, seeming the most confident of the three, and Bush gets a small bump in the polls as a result.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950124,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940124,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Ueberroth’s unknown political positions allow him to be vague, and it works to his advantage. Though Gore and Quayle try to chase Pete down, he’s able to deflect from them and give a great endorsement of everything you have done, the debate is seen as a draw between him and Gore. Quayle, of course, is the clear loser.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950125,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940125,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"Brown’s energetic persona proves to be a boon, being a stark contrast to the wooden appearances of Gore and Quayle. He gets cornered on his Konami Code of a belief system a few times, but he pulls through, and is seen as the winner by a small majority of the people watching.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950126,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940126,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"It's a performance like no one had ever seen. Lee completely destroys both Gore and Quayle, asserting himself early while aggressively peddling his populist platitudes. He even gets a few one-liners off against Gore that prove to be mightily popular with the media outlets afterward. You roll your eyes, but at least it worked.\"}},{\"model\":\"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\",\"pk\":950127,\"fields\":{\"answer\":940127,\"candidate\":154,\"answer_feedback\":\"“No” Perot simply replies. Nearly 2 years of hard work, countless hours of lost sleep, tens of thousands of miles on your beat up truck, thousands of minutes speaking, hundreds of hours pouring over ads, dozens of phone calls to Perot, all shattered with a single word.<br><br>Dejected, you turn off the TV, and go to bed. Its time to get the fuck out of this country.\"}}]");

